Symmetrical small joint polyarthritis, Morning stiffness >1 hour, Sparing of DIP joints

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-052
Reason for Visit: Acute, functionally paralyzing flare of symmetrical joint pain and massive swelling in the hands, rendering the patient unable to perform basic morning ADLs.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing a severe, acute-on-chronic exacerbation of polyarticular joint swelling and agonizing stiffness lasting for hours, resulting in total functional loss of the hands. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out a superimposed acute polyarticular septic process, and to administer immediate, potent systemic anti-inflammatory therapy to halt profound autoimmune tissue destruction and restore basic functional capacity prior to daytime rheumatological review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hands are swollen like hot balloons; they are locked solid, and I haven't been able to dress myself or open a pill bottle all morning."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of symmetrical polyarthritis. Timeline: Insidious onset of joint stiffness over the past 3 months; dramatically worsened over the last 48 hours into a severe, immobilizing flare affecting the small joints of both hands and wrists. The patient reports profound morning stiffness today that has lasted for over three hours without improvement. Complains of pronounced systemic fatigue, malaise, and feeling generally unwell (low-grade feverish). The affected joints are exquisitely tender, preventing any grip or fine motor tasks. Patient notes a recent history of waking at night due to numbness and tingling in the right hand (suspected secondary carpal tunnel syndrome driven by wrist synovitis).
Functional Status: Acutely impaired; bilateral hands are functionally useless due to severe pain, swelling, and stiffness; unable to dress, feed, or medicate independently.
Social/Safety Concerns: Complete loss of fine motor autonomy; isolated at home.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/84 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, looking systemically fatigued and unwell, holding both hands elevated and perfectly still.
Physical Exam: Bilateral upper extremities reveal severe, symmetrical polyarthritis predominantly affecting the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints, as well as the wrists. The involved joints are boggy, swollen, erythematous, and exquisitely tender to light palpation, indicating active, massive synovial proliferation. Crucially, there is strict sparing of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints. A positive squeeze test elicits significant, agonizing pain across the MCP rows bilaterally. Firm, non-tender subcutaneous rheumatoid nodules are palpable over the proximal ulnar extensor surfaces. Tinel's sign is positive at the right volar wrist. No large joint (knee/hip) effusions currently.
Environmental Observations: Unopened medication bottles and half-dressed appearance confirm the profound functional deficit.
Medication Reconciliation: Unable to open regular NSAID blister packs today due to the severe functional loss in the hands.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute, severe flare of Rheumatoid Arthritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Viral Polyarthritis (e.g., Parvovirus B19) - Can mimic RA exactly, but the presence of chronic rheumatoid nodules and the insidious 3-month history strongly favor a primary autoimmune etiology rather than an acute viral illness.

Osteoarthritis (Erosive) - Ruled out clinically by the presence of boggy, warm synovial swelling (rather than hard bony osteophytes), the profound >1 hour morning stiffness, and the strict sparing of the DIP joints (which are primarily affected in OA).

Polyarticular Gout/Pseudogout - Less likely given the symmetrical, small-joint presentation, presence of rheumatoid nodules, and the prominent systemic features (prolonged morning stiffness, severe fatigue).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a classic, severe manifestation of active Rheumatoid Arthritis, characterized by explosive, symmetrical small-joint synovitis, profound morning stiffness, and systemic malaise. The critical clinical task was differentiating this highly inflammatory autoimmune flare from mechanical osteoarthritis or a viral syndrome. The physical exam perfectly matched the RA criteria (MCP/PIP involvement, DIP sparing, positive squeeze test). The severity of the swelling and stiffness resulted in total functional paralysis of the hands, rendering the patient helpless and in agony. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the autoimmune nature of the crisis, bypassed inappropriate mechanical treatments, and allowed for the immediate initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids to rapidly extinguish the aggressive joint destruction.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT to rapidly decrease acute systemic inflammation. Prescribed oral Prednisolone 25mg daily for 5 days as a short bridging course to halt the severe synovitis, alongside ongoing Naproxen 500mg BD.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Take the prescribed steroid medication exactly at 6:00 AM with food. Consequence: The medication suppresses the peak immune system attack that happens overnight, drastically reducing the "concrete" morning stiffness and allowing you to use your hands to dress yourself (positive reinforcement).
Action: Submerge both hands in a basin of warm water for 15 minutes immediately upon waking. Consequence: Increased local blood flow softens the thickened, inflamed joint capsules, physically loosening the severe morning stiffness and restoring basic finger movement (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to a Rheumatologist for initiation or escalation of Disease-Modifying Antirheumatic Drugs (DMARDs).
DME/Safety (Optional): Assisted patient in opening all necessary daily medication blister packs and placing them in an accessible dosette box.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3-5 days to monitor response to steroids, order formal serology (RF, anti-CCP), and expedite rheumatology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden onset of a high spiking fever with uncontrollable shivering, if one single joint becomes massively larger, redder, and hotter than all the others (risk of superimposed septic joint), or if there is sudden difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically overwhelming for RA, supported by the nodules and joint pattern. Blood tests (RF, anti-CCP) and X-rays are required for formal rheumatological staging. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The massive swelling and stiffness will improve dramatically within 48 hours on the steroids. However, this is a destructive, chronic disease that requires powerful, long-term immune-suppressing medications to prevent permanent joint deformity.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you develop a high fever and chills, or if one specific joint suddenly becomes much worse, hotter, and redder than the rest.

